1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,000
POV: your agent forgot again.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:11,500
Folder B, dammit—how MANY times do I have to tell you, man?! It's 34 employees. I HAVE 34 EMPLOYEES, MAN! Why am I always reminding youuuu—

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Stop it. Get some help.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Have you ever rage-quit a coding session? Flipped an agent OFF… and back ON? Join the club, buddy.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Our agents have a memory problem. Two weeks later, it's like we never talked.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
So this is how my girlfriend feels.

7
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Every agent wakes up stateless. No history. No yesterday. All that intelligence — and zero continuity.

8
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:42,000
But people tell me the fix is simple…

9
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:49,000
'Just get a 6-million-billion context window, bro.' Except that isn't memory. It's a TAX. And I can't solve that —

10
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:53,000
— that's big-lab work. I mean, I would, but —

11
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:00,000
— my Meta money hasn't landed yet. So until then? I'll just put the fries in the bag. So what CAN I do?

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,000
I started with three questions. First: my girlfriend mentioned her friend's dog once — weeks later, still there. Why does THAT stick?

13
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Second: how does a memory show up exactly when I need it, when I never went looking for it?

14
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Third: how is it all wired together — so one memory drags the next one up with it?

15
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Fifty-four papers later, here’s how the mind actually does it:

16
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,000
In the brain, predictive coding throws out what's expected. We built the Observer: a surprise gate that keeps what's new.

17
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:32,000
In the brain, memory is tiered into moments, facts, and lessons. We built the Scribe: it sorts tiers and links a live graph.

18
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:35,500
And when you recall, cues spread activation across the network. We built the Retriever: a 400-token Card stamped with WHY.

19
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:38,000
RAG didn't die—it finally got a real job.

20
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:43,000
Simulate the brain's solutions. Never simulate its constraints. We didn't copy the forgetting. We copied the finding.

21
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:47,000
All theory. Backend servers humming in the dark. How do you WATCH a memory form? Build a brain you can look at.

22
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Tell it a rule once. Watch the right side file it: checks if it's new, links it to the graph. Done.

23
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Truth changes mid-project: 'I'm vegetarian.' It doesn't duplicate or hallucinate. It supersedes the old fact, non-destructively.

24
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:05,000
New session, zero context. Give it a command: 'Implement feature A.' Activation spreads, pulling the decisions, conventions, and rules.

25
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:09,000
Take the memory away… and it's a goldfish: 'I don't know.'

26
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,000
Flip it back ON… and the genius is back.

27
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,000
Then it sleeps. Replays the day's chaos and compresses messy moments into clean lessons.

28
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:24,000
Now—how do you actually test if an agent's memory works? I built an automated test harness: a second agent to interrogate the first.

29
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
And because the whole memory is an explicit graph, when it's wrong, you trace the exact path to see where it failed.

30
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Built on Meta: powered by Muse Spark via the Meta Model API, shipped as one universal MCP server.

31
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:45,000
The dev whose IDE knows their stack. Personal assistants that remember you. And yes… the one thing that matters: her birthday.

32
00:02:45,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Stop treating AI like an amnesiac. Give it a mind that persists. Tottie. Built on Meta. Build for your world.
